  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n07z/2am surface analysis shows a slow-moving cold front\nextending from West Virginia to southern Missouri while a 1030mb\nhigh pressure is anchored over Ontario. An influx of cooler/drier\nair from the northeast has led to mostly dry conditions north \nof the I-72 corridor early this morning. However further south, \nthe nocturnal low-level jet continues to interact with the \ndeparting boundary to trigger clusters of showers and \nthunderstorms. The precipitation has been most widespread\nalong/south of a Jacksonville to Mattoon line, and this will\ncontinue to be the case for the next couple of hours before the\nbest forcing shifts E/SE into the Ohio River Valley and the\nshowers taper off toward dawn. Despite a prominent decrease in\nareal coverage, think widely scattered showers will continue to\nbe possible throughout the day as an upper low spinning over \nNebraska slowly pivots eastward. Forecast soundings suggest the \nlow overcast currently in place will persist as well, resulting \nin a cloudy and cool day. High temperatures will range from the \nmiddle to upper 60s along/north of I-72 to the upper 70s far \nsoutheast around Flora and Lawrenceville. \n\nIsolated showers will persist tonight into Tuesday morning as \nthe upper low gradually shifts into north-central Illinois. Once\nthis feature pushes to the east, synoptic subsidence on its \nback side will lead to an end to the very light precip and \npartial clearing by Tuesday afternoon. Highs on Tuesday will be\nsimilar to today, ranging from the middle 60s far north to the\nmiddle 70s south of I-70. As skies clear and winds diminish,\nradiational cooling will lead to the coolest night of the\nforecast period Tuesday night. Overnight lows will drop into the\nlower to middle 50s, but may dip into the upper 40s in a few\nlow-lying rural locations west of I-57. Probabilities for temps\nbelow 50 degrees have lowered across east-central Illinois \nwhere partial cloud cover and a lingering breeze may limit \ncooling: however, the 00z NBM still indicates a 50-60% chance \nnorthwest of the Illinois River.\n\nAfter that, the departing upper low is progged to linger over the\nMid-Atlantic...creating a blocking pattern across the eastern \nCONUS. As a result, upper heights will gradually rise as ridging\nbuilds over the Midwest by the end of the week through early \nnext week. The end result will be mostly dry weather with slowly\nwarming temperatures climbing back into the upper 70s/lower 80s\nby next Sunday/Monday.",
